WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.395 --> 00:00:06.660
BÁLINT PATÓ: Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.210
Welcome to our talk.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:10.280
In this session,
we'll show you how

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:11.810
you can develop
your applications

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:16.970
in IDEs using Cloud Code if
you're deploying to Cloud Run.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:18.800
My name is Bálint Pató.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.000
I'm a software
engineer at Google.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.330
And I was the lead for the
project delivering the Cloud

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:25.897
Run experience for Cloud Code.

00:00:25.897 --> 00:00:27.230
MATTHEW MICHIHARA: And I'm Matt.

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:30.890
I'm also an engineer at
Google on the Cloud Code team.

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:33.590
BÁLINT PATÓ: Before
we jump into the IDEs,

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:36.290
for those who are
unfamiliar with Cloud Run,

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:39.980
let's see briefly what
the Cloud Run actually is.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:44.270
Cloud Run enables you to
run stateless containers

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:46.430
in a fully managed environment.

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.950
These stateless
container instances

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.500
are spun up when the
application endpoint is

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.540
triggered on demand.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:55.680
If the application
receives no requests,

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.840
however, these instances
scale back down to zero.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.790
Depending on what
your preference is,

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:05.090
you have two flavors of Cloud
Run that you can work with.

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:07.280
The fully managed
environment is a pay

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:10.370
as you go service with
zero need for managing

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:13.610
any kind of infrastructure,
meaning no servers,

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:15.500
no clusters are required.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:18.440
However, it is a
cloud-only option.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.850
Cloud Run for Anthos runs Cloud
Run workloads on GKE clusters,

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.410
enabling customers
to run workloads

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:28.730
wherever Anthos can run, for
example, in your data center,

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:30.030
on premises.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:31.850
Cloud Run is
typically used to run

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:37.400
web applications, public APIs,
or backends for mobile apps.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.430
You can choose to make these
stateless containers, however,

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:42.380
private as well--

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:45.610
as part of a larger
application, for example,

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:51.290
microservice or executing
tasks based on events or part

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:54.070
of your ETL pipeline.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:56.760
This all sounds great in theory.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.840
However, it is actually easier
to show what Cloud Run is

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.770
than explaining it.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:05.700
So let's deploy a sample
app using the Cloud

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:08.370
Console and the command line.

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.430
To deploy an application to
Cloud Run from my browser,

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.860
I'm going to use
Google Cloud Console.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:21.960
Let's choose the Cloud Run
tab, Create Service button.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.430
My choice of
deployment platform is

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:27.370
going to be the fully
managed Cloud Run service.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:31.980
And I'm going to deploy
it to us-central1 region.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:36.945
The service name is
something simple.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.415
I'm going into let anybody
access this application.

00:02:44.415 --> 00:02:47.620
It's going to be public.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:51.250
And for the
application, I'm going

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:53.680
to use a prepared,
pre-built application

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.210
that is just a simple web
app listening on port 8080,

00:02:58.210 --> 00:02:59.170
and that's it.

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:01.900
I'm just going to hit
Create, and Cloud Run

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:04.520
will deploy my application.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:08.080
So at any time I
hit the endpoint,

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.040
the response is going to be
coming from an instance that

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:16.310
was spun up on demand.

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:18.320
And there it is.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.490
So let's do something
similar on the command line

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:24.030
starting from code.

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:29.260
So I have a simple
Go application

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:33.710
that uses a Docker file to
build an image out of it.

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:34.850
So let's build it.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.930
And I'm going to
push it as well.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:47.150
"docker push" to this GCR--

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:49.760
Google Container Registry--

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:58.760
URL in my project, so
that "gcloud run deploy"

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:00.500
can deploy it.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:04.000
I'm going to go through a
very similar set of choices.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.140
So the fully managed
platform is my choice

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:07.610
of deployment platform.

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:09.180
Service name is going to be--

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:12.180
let's name it goapp123.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:16.110
And the us-central--
going to be the region.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:21.240
And my application is
going to be yes, public.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:24.990
G Cloud does the same thing
that Cloud Console did.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:29.940
It asks Cloud Run to deploy
this container image,

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:33.640
so when I hit this
endpoint, my new instance

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:38.270
is going to respond,
write "Hello, world!"

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:39.860
That was great.

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:42.020
Isn't Cloud Run just awesome?

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:43.520
As soon as you have
a container, you

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:45.560
can just run it in the cloud.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:48.280
Fantastic.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:51.940
But then the questions come,
how do we get to that container?

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:55.060
Also, do I always need
to use the command

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:57.130
line or the browser?

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:01.490
Luckily, that is exactly what
we're going to talk about here.

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:03.400
So let's have a
look at the agenda.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.730
We covered what Cloud Run is.

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:10.450
Next, Matt will explain
what Cloud Code is.

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:14.140
And then we'll show you
a bunch of demos starting

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:18.730
from the IDEs, where we
will deploy from VSCode,

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:22.670
and we'll develop a more
complex application in IntelliJ.

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:24.183
On to you, Matt.

00:05:24.183 --> 00:05:25.600
MATTHEW MICHIHARA:
Thanks, Bálint.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.640
Cloud Run is great,
but developing

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:30.490
for it can be challenging
without the right tools.

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:32.980
For example, if you're new
to Cloud Run, getting started

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:34.840
might seem a little
intimidating.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:37.960
It can be difficult to know
how to build a container.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:40.710
Once your application is
containerized for Cloud Run,

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:42.318
developing and
debugging it locally

00:05:42.318 --> 00:05:43.860
is not as straightforward
as it would

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:47.550
be with a non-containerized
application.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:50.373
Finally, there are many tools
to assist with Cloud Run,

00:05:50.373 --> 00:05:52.290
but it would be nice not
to have to constantly

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:56.410
context switch between the
CLI, web browser, and editor.

00:05:56.410 --> 00:05:58.480
This is where a
Cloud Code comes in.

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:00.790
Cloud Code is a set
of tools for IntelliJ

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:02.470
and Visual Studio
Code that helps

00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:04.720
you write cloud-native
applications, including

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:06.490
applications for Cloud Run.

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:10.660
Cloud Code is integrated with
sample projects in Java, Go,

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:13.570
Python, and Node.js to help
you get started setting up

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:15.357
your Cloud Run application.

00:06:15.357 --> 00:06:17.440
Cloud Code can help you
containerize your existing

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:20.050
application and will simulate
the Cloud Run environment

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:20.992
locally.

00:06:20.992 --> 00:06:23.200
This lets you develop your
app in an environment that

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:26.080
is very close to production,
while letting you use your IDE

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:29.810
to do things like set
breakpoints and debug.

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:31.700
Once you're ready to
deploy, Cloud Code

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:34.820
makes that easy too, by
hooking into the IDE's existing

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:36.290
deployment mechanisms.

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:38.120
And you can view
previous deployments

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:40.130
in the Cloud Run Explorer.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:42.262
Cloud Code is available
for free for Visual Studio

00:06:42.262 --> 00:06:45.230
Code and the JetBrains IDEs
from the respective plugin

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:46.470
marketplaces.

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:48.172
We have public issue
trackers on GitHub,

00:06:48.172 --> 00:06:50.630
and we'd love to hear if you
have any feedback after trying

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:52.140
it out.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:53.820
Now on to the fun stuff.

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:56.500
Bálint is going to show you how
to get started with Cloud Code

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:58.420
for VSCode.

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:01.060
BÁLINT PATÓ: Remember when I
asked how you can deploy an app

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:04.780
from the IDE without the use
of the browser or the command

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:05.770
line?

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:09.100
Well, let's see our
first demo from the IDE.

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:12.840
I have VSCode installed,
and that has IntelliJ.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:16.320
So I'm going to show a
VSCode-based demo first.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:19.640
In this demo, we'll see how you
can create a simple application

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:22.870
and deploy it to Cloud Run using
Cloud Code for Visual Studio

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:23.860
Code.

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:27.010
After you install the extension
from the VSCode marketplace,

00:07:27.010 --> 00:07:30.070
you can confirm that it is
activated by seeing a Cloud

00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:33.190
Code button in the status bar.

00:07:33.190 --> 00:07:35.950
Let's start by creating a
new sample application first.

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:37.810
And we'll click on
the Cloud Code button

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:39.580
to bring up the
available commands,

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:45.730
then hit New Application
and Cloud Run application.

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:48.670
The available languages for
sample applications are Java,

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:51.940
Python, Node.js, Golang, and C#.

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:54.210
I will create my sample
application written in Go.

00:07:57.760 --> 00:07:59.470
My application is created now.

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:03.670
It is a simple web application
that listens on port 8080.

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:06.180
It has a Docker file.

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:10.540
It has a simple Go
file and a test file.

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:13.870
And in the README, we can
see that the next step

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:17.670
is to open the command
palette and run the Cloud Code

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:19.590
deployment command.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:20.750
So let's do that.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:25.310
I'm going to enter "Cloud
Code deploy," and there it is.

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:33.059
Cloud Code recognizes
that I'm logged into GCP

00:08:33.059 --> 00:08:34.890
with my account using G Cloud.

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:36.450
It automatically
selects the project

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:38.520
that I set as the default one.

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.640
Let's walk through
the options to deploy.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:44.850
Most of these should be very
familiar from the Cloud Console

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:46.860
options that I demoed earlier.

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:49.650
Let's start with the
Service Settings.

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:52.380
Cloud could allows me to create
a new revision of an existing

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:55.320
service or to create
a new service.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:09:00.494
I will create a new service and
name it hello-nextonair-world.

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:11.880
As I explained, we
have the two flavors

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:13.600
of the deployment platform--

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:15.300
for Cloud Run,
the fully managed,

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:16.668
and Cloud Run for Anthos.

00:09:16.668 --> 00:09:18.210
I am going to deploy
to fully managed

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:21.598
using the us-central1 region.

00:09:21.598 --> 00:09:23.265
I'm going to keep my
application public.

00:09:26.230 --> 00:09:30.790
And the Revision Settings
show the opportunity

00:09:30.790 --> 00:09:34.250
for setting some advanced
settings for my application,

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:36.730
including the container image
URL, where the application is

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:44.240
going to be pushed up to, and
the other advanced options.

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:47.840
The Build Settings
are something that

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:50.480
is different from the
Cloud Console settings.

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:56.270
This is what sets up how the
application is going to be

00:09:56.270 --> 00:10:00.780
built into a Docker image.

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:03.270
Cloud Code recognizes that
my application can be built

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:06.683
using Docker or Buildpacks.

00:10:06.683 --> 00:10:08.600
I'm going to choose
Buildpacks, because that's

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:10.060
the cooler option.

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:15.700
Because I don't have to write a
single line of Docker file code

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:20.020
or know what Docker is, it
just builds my application

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:21.710
into a Docker image.

00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:24.750
So let's hit Deploy.

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:27.710
As my application
builds and deploys,

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:32.950
I should be able to
see, after it succeeds,

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:38.320
that my new service should
show up between my projects.

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:42.400
Cloud Code provides
a Cloud Run panel

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:47.500
that has a Cloud Run
explorer which, as we see,

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:51.560
shows the list of
services in my project.

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:54.890
Hello-nextonair-world,
my newly created service,

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:55.730
just showed up.

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:00.880
We can see in the
Properties panel

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:03.880
that it has the service
name hello-nextonair-world.

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:06.010
It has a URL, where
it's deployed,

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:08.470
us-central region, as expected.

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:11.080
And it has one
serving revision which

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:14.500
has 100% of the traffic
allocated to it.

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:20.510
I can check the logs
for this revision.

00:11:20.510 --> 00:11:23.030
And by right-clicking
on the service,

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:26.660
I can also open the
URL for my application.

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:28.130
And there it is.

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:31.580
Before we continue, I wanted
to add two more notes.

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:33.380
Local development
features are going

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:35.690
to be released later
in August, and they

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:39.900
will be available in
both VSCode and IntelliJ.

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:43.770
I did not demo them, because we
didn't have time for it here.

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:47.790
However, Matt will show
you, inside IntelliJ,

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:50.530
the local development features.

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:54.480
The second note is about
the Artifact Registry.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:56.720
While we are showing
Google Container Registry

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:59.880
in our demos, I recommend
you to check out

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:03.390
Google Artifact Registry,
which is currently in beta.

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:06.150
And it will provide the
next generation of features

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:08.670
for storing, amongst
other artifacts,

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:11.903
Docker containers as well.

00:12:11.903 --> 00:12:13.320
MATTHEW MICHIHARA:
Thanks, Bálint.

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:14.730
Next, I'll be
showing you how you

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:18.910
can do some local development
using Cloud Code for IntelliJ.

00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:20.680
In this next demo,
I'm going to walk

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:22.950
through how we can update
an existing Cloud Run

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:25.840
application to talk to
an external database.

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:28.690
We'll then iterate on
some changes, debug it,

00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:31.270
and finally, deploy
it back to Cloud Run.

00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:33.130
Here's an application
currently deployed

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:35.890
to Cloud Run that lets you vote
on your preferred indentation

00:12:35.890 --> 00:12:38.800
preferences, tabs or spaces.

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:40.990
As you can see here, this
app keeps a running count

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:43.960
of votes for tabs and spaces
and shows Recent Votes

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:46.360
down at the bottom here.

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:48.580
Each time I vote,
my vote is recorded,

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:51.580
and the Recent Votes
section is updated.

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:53.740
Now I'll switch to
Cloud Code for IntelliJ,

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:55.810
where we can examine the code.

00:12:55.810 --> 00:12:58.270
It's a Node.js Express
app, and the bulk of it

00:12:58.270 --> 00:13:02.370
is implemented in
this server.js file.

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:05.560
Now, as you can see here, there
are two main route handlers--

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:08.140
one for handling the
GET HTTP request and one

00:13:08.140 --> 00:13:10.870
for handling the
POST HTTP request.

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:14.380
So in the GET request handler,
we get the recent votes.

00:13:14.380 --> 00:13:18.040
We get the counts for the number
of votes for tabs and spaces.

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:19.690
We do some calculations
to determine

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:23.730
what text to display on the
screen, and then we render it.

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:27.480
In the Post request handler,
we receive an incoming vote,

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:29.370
and then we use
this castVote method

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:31.480
to record the incoming vote.

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:34.650
I'll step into this
castVote method, where

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:36.630
it simply takes the
vote and persists it

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:38.730
to our in-memory "votes" array.

00:13:38.730 --> 00:13:43.030
So here is what the current
architecture looks like.

00:13:43.030 --> 00:13:44.730
So there's a problem with this.

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:47.430
As you can see here, the way
we implemented our data store

00:13:47.430 --> 00:13:49.530
is in-memory, inside
the container.

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:51.240
And this is a problem,
because Cloud Run

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:52.770
is designed to work
with stateless,

00:13:52.770 --> 00:13:54.450
ephemeral containers.

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:57.900
So when Cloud Run scales
down to zero or scales up,

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:00.000
this data will be lost.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.730
We can avoid this data loss and
make our container stateless

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:04.920
by moving our data store
out of the container

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:07.960
and by having our container
talk to an external database.

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:10.440
So let's try to implement
this with Cloud SQL, which

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:13.690
is GCP's managed SQL database.

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:16.662
Here is what our desired
architecture will look like.

00:14:16.662 --> 00:14:19.120
I've gone ahead and created
that "votes" table in the Cloud

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:22.870
Console and updated the code
to talk to that database.

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:25.150
You can see that I replaced
the methods here with ones

00:14:25.150 --> 00:14:27.310
that execute SQL queries.

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:28.690
For full details
on the changes I

00:14:28.690 --> 00:14:30.310
made, check out the
code in the GitHub

00:14:30.310 --> 00:14:32.930
repo linked in the slides.

00:14:32.930 --> 00:14:34.620
So we just made
a lot of changes,

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:36.530
and I could push this
directly to Cloud Run

00:14:36.530 --> 00:14:39.380
to see if it works, but
I don't want to do that.

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:42.050
Normally, I'd want to test
my changes out locally first

00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:44.600
before deploying to production.

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:47.750
Cloud Code lets me do that by
providing a simulated Cloud Run

00:14:47.750 --> 00:14:49.800
local development
environment to test on.

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:52.280
So let's try that out
using the Cloud Run "Run

00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:54.890
locally" run configuration.

00:14:54.890 --> 00:14:57.950
I have one set up here that
I'll quickly run through.

00:14:57.950 --> 00:15:00.080
I've named it "Run
locally" and configured

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:02.510
it to build my application
using the Docker builders,

00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:05.630
since there is a Docker
file present in my project.

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:08.330
Cloud Code has also detected
that my project is buildable

00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:10.970
using Buildpacks, which is a
container-building technology

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:12.650
that will automatically
containerize

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:15.478
my application without the
need for a Docker file.

00:15:15.478 --> 00:15:18.020
But since we have a Docker file,
we can just stick with that.

00:15:18.020 --> 00:15:19.760
In the Advanced
revision settings

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:21.530
here, I've also
configured my application

00:15:21.530 --> 00:15:24.550
with the environment variables,
SQL connection string,

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:28.280
and service account required to
talk to the Cloud SQL database.

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:30.690
Full details on these are
also provided in the link

00:15:30.690 --> 00:15:32.390
to GitHub repository.

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:33.710
So let's try it out.

00:15:33.710 --> 00:15:36.590
Clicking Play here will start
the Cloud Code simulated Cloud

00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:38.540
Run environment.

00:15:38.540 --> 00:15:40.310
Internally, Cloud
Code is using tools

00:15:40.310 --> 00:15:43.580
like Skaffold and Minikube to
build, containerize, and run

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:45.500
my application in
a local Kubernetes

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:47.360
cluster behind the scenes.

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:50.000
All right, it looks like
it was able to run my app.

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:51.770
We can launch it by
going to the Event Log

00:15:51.770 --> 00:15:54.820
here and clicking this link.

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:56.850
And here's my app
running locally.

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:59.230
You can see it's running on
localhost in the Cloud Run

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:01.043
simulated environment.

00:16:01.043 --> 00:16:03.210
I can cast votes, and it
looks like they're properly

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:03.972
persisted.

00:16:03.972 --> 00:16:05.680
But let's just double
check to make sure,

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:08.790
and I can do that by
going back to my terminal

00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:12.360
and checking to see if they're
actually saved to Cloud SQL.

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:17.140
So I'm going to quickly write
a SQL query to verify this.

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:19.440
And yep, it looks like the
votes I cast were properly

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:20.880
saved in the votes table.

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:22.200
So that's great.

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:25.230
To recap, Cloud Code helped
me containerize and run

00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:27.525
my application in a simulated
Cloud Run environment

00:16:27.525 --> 00:16:29.580
so that I could test
my application locally

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:31.580
before deploying the Cloud Run.

00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:33.450
When I'm developing
a Cloud Run app,

00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:35.400
I want quick feedback loops.

00:16:35.400 --> 00:16:37.680
I want to make changes and
see those changes reflected

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:39.575
quickly so that I can iterate.

00:16:39.575 --> 00:16:41.700
And I don't want to have
to manually build an image

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:43.500
and redeploy to
Cloud Run each time

00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:45.790
I want to test my
application out.

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:47.940
Fortunately, Cloud Code
makes that easy for me

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:50.400
by automatically building
and redeploying my app

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:52.650
to a local simulated
Cloud Run environment

00:16:52.650 --> 00:16:55.300
when it detects source changes.

00:16:55.300 --> 00:16:57.640
So let's see that in action.

00:16:57.640 --> 00:17:00.360
Let's say I want to add
some extra exclamation

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:03.510
points to the end of this text.

00:17:03.510 --> 00:17:06.900
I'll go to my IDE and add
some exclamation points.

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:09.359
When I hit Save, Cloud
Code detect the change

00:17:09.359 --> 00:17:12.902
and automatically rebuilds
and redeploys my container.

00:17:12.902 --> 00:17:14.819
This keeps the inner
development loop tight so

00:17:14.819 --> 00:17:16.839
that I can quickly iterate.

00:17:16.839 --> 00:17:20.010
All right, so now if I go back
to my app and refresh the page,

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:22.105
my change is reflected there.

00:17:22.105 --> 00:17:24.480
Notice how I didn't need to
craft and run multiple Docker

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:26.550
commands in order to
redeploy my changes,

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:29.490
since Cloud Code automatically
took care of that for me.

00:17:29.490 --> 00:17:32.220
Let's see how Cloud Code
helps me debug my Cloud Run

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:33.900
application.

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:36.090
Normally, debugging code
running inside containers

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:37.580
can be tricky.

00:17:37.580 --> 00:17:40.080
There are source mappings to
configure and ports to forward,

00:17:40.080 --> 00:17:43.140
and each language handles
it a bit differently.

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:45.090
Cloud Code takes care
of all of this for me

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:47.460
by utilizing Skaffold's
debugging capabilities

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:48.780
under the hood.

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:51.750
My application has a
bug that I've planted.

00:17:51.750 --> 00:17:56.590
If spaces start winning,
their win count goes negative.

00:17:56.590 --> 00:17:59.480
So Cloud Code lets me debug this
using all the debugging tools

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:00.650
that I'm used to.

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:03.470
I don't have to modify my
Docker file to include debugging

00:18:03.470 --> 00:18:05.450
utilities or configure ports--

00:18:05.450 --> 00:18:07.620
Cloud Code does it for me.

00:18:07.620 --> 00:18:11.120
I can set a breakpoint,
and then run my application

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:13.240
in debug mode.

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:15.540
So right now, Cloud Code
is configuring my container

00:18:15.540 --> 00:18:16.980
to be debuggable.

00:18:16.980 --> 00:18:21.210
It also detected the appropriate
path mapping for me to set up.

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:25.440
Now that the debugger is
attached, if I reload the page,

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:28.860
the breakpoint is hit,
and I can step through.

00:18:28.860 --> 00:18:30.930
So I'll step through--

00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:33.370
that things look right.

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:35.920
And here is where that
negative 1 came from.

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:38.110
"diff" is negative
1, and it looks

00:18:38.110 --> 00:18:41.650
like it's because I swapped
tabCount and spaceCount.

00:18:41.650 --> 00:18:44.120
tabCount should be
subtracted from spaceCount.

00:18:44.120 --> 00:18:47.090
So I'm going to
fix that right now.

00:18:47.090 --> 00:18:53.160
Save, and rerun this to
verify that it was fixed.

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:54.860
OK, my application is running.

00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:55.990
So if I reload the page--

00:19:00.162 --> 00:19:02.120
great, it looks like it's
not negative anymore,

00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:03.690
and it's showing us
the right number.

00:19:03.690 --> 00:19:05.680
So this looks good.

00:19:05.680 --> 00:19:08.770
Cloud Code just helped me
debug my Cloud Run application

00:19:08.770 --> 00:19:10.600
locally using all
the normal debugging

00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:12.230
tools that I would
with IntelliJ,

00:19:12.230 --> 00:19:14.230
without requiring me to
go through a complicated

00:19:14.230 --> 00:19:16.530
configuration setup.

00:19:16.530 --> 00:19:18.660
At this point, I feel
comfortable deploying my app

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:21.170
to Cloud Run on GCP.

00:19:21.170 --> 00:19:23.930
So Cloud Code lets me do
that by using its deploy run

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:25.620
configuration.

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:28.890
This time I'm using the Cloud
Code Cloud Run deploy run

00:19:28.890 --> 00:19:30.180
configuration.

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:32.900
Here, I've gone ahead
and set that up.

00:19:32.900 --> 00:19:34.790
I've selected the
GCP projects that I'm

00:19:34.790 --> 00:19:36.860
going to deploy this to.

00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:39.840
Next, I configured the
deployment platform,

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:42.290
and I can choose between
fully managed and Cloud

00:19:42.290 --> 00:19:44.180
Run for Anthos.

00:19:44.180 --> 00:19:47.120
Fully managed will have GCP
manage all the infrastructure

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:49.640
for me, whereas
Cloud run for Anthos

00:19:49.640 --> 00:19:52.970
will let me run my Cloud Run
application on a GKE cluster,

00:19:52.970 --> 00:19:54.500
for example.

00:19:54.500 --> 00:19:56.180
I'm going to stick
with fully managed,

00:19:56.180 --> 00:20:00.470
and I'm going to deploy
this to the us-west1 region.

00:20:00.470 --> 00:20:03.340
Here, the service name is what
my Cloud Run app is called,

00:20:03.340 --> 00:20:05.316
so I've named it "vote."

00:20:05.316 --> 00:20:08.930
And next, I configured the
Authentication settings.

00:20:08.930 --> 00:20:12.410
I can either have this "Allow
unauthenticated invocations"

00:20:12.410 --> 00:20:14.330
or "Require authentication."

00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:16.790
And since I want this to be
a publicly available service,

00:20:16.790 --> 00:20:20.272
I opted for allowing
unauthenticated invocations.

00:20:20.272 --> 00:20:21.980
This field configures
where the container

00:20:21.980 --> 00:20:23.820
images I'm building get stored.

00:20:23.820 --> 00:20:26.030
So I'm going to stick with
Google Container Registry,

00:20:26.030 --> 00:20:28.160
which is the default here.

00:20:28.160 --> 00:20:30.140
I also filled out these
environment variables

00:20:30.140 --> 00:20:34.920
that I did for the local
run [? rec ?] configuration.

00:20:34.920 --> 00:20:36.940
And last, I configured
the builder settings

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:40.210
to build my container
using Docker.

00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:44.050
So now if I hit Run on the
Cloud Code Cloud Run deploy run

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:47.320
configuration, it's
going to build my app,

00:20:47.320 --> 00:20:49.950
containerize it, and deploy
it to Cloud Run on GCP.

00:20:52.820 --> 00:20:55.940
Great, so it looks like my
application finished deploying.

00:20:55.940 --> 00:20:58.850
If I click on the link
that it printed out for me,

00:20:58.850 --> 00:21:02.510
it looks like it
deployed successfully,

00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:05.870
and I can still cast votes.

00:21:05.870 --> 00:21:07.880
So to recap, Cloud
Code just helped

00:21:07.880 --> 00:21:10.640
me iterate on my
application, debug it,

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:12.510
and finally, deploy
it back to Cloud Run.

